Ichthyosaurs were aquatic dinosaurs with dorsal fins and tails, similar to fish, but their closest relatives were terrestrial reptiles without dorsal fins or tails, illustrating convergent evolution. Convergent evolution occurs when two unrelated species develop similar traits independently in response to similar environmental pressures, as seen in the similarity between the pectoral fins of fish and the front legs of cats. This process happens when different species adapt to similar environments and develop similar morphological traits despite distinct evolutionary paths.
