The key difference between starch and cellulose is the shape of the bonds linking their glucose monomers. The bonds in starch are in a straight alpha-1,4-glycosidic shape, allowing the human body to break them down with amylase. In contrast, the bonds in cellulose are in a beta-1,4-glycosidic shape, which the human digestive system cannot break down due to a lack of specific enzymes.
