rst codon position.', 'The change with the greatest deleterious effect is a single nucleotide substitution of the nucleotide occupying the first codon position, which is most likely to result in a premature stop codon. This is because the first position of a codon determines the identity of the amino acid, and a substitution can lead to a non-synonymous mutation.', 'A single nucleotide substitution in the first position of a codon is likely to result in a stop codon, leading to premature protein termination. This can cause a complete loss of protein function. The resulting protein will be truncated and non-functional.', 'The mutation may alter the enzymes substrate specificity by changing the shape of the protein, which can affect the binding of substrates. The nonpolar amino acid may alter the hydrophobic interactions with the substrate, changing the enzymes shape and specificity. This change can lead to a loss of substrate specificity.', 'A single nucleotide substitution in the first codon position would have a significant impact, as it would likely alter the start codon, potentially disrupting the entire translation process.', 'The mutation may alter the enzymes substrate specificity by changing the shape of the protein. The substitution of a polar amino acid with a nonpolar one can alter the proteins conformation, affecting its binding properties.', 'The change is a single nucleotide substitution. It affects the first position of the codon, which determines the amino acid. This has the greatest potential for a deleterious effect.', 'A single nucleotide substitution in the first position of the start codon would have the greatest deleterious effect, as it could lead to a premature stop codon and a truncated, non-functional protein. This is because the first position of the start codon determines the amino acid and the entire reading frame of the protein.', 'A single nucleotide substitution in the first position of the first codon has the greatest deleterious effe