Homologous structures are similar structures in different species that evolve from a common ancestor and serve as evidence for natural selection. Examples include the forelegs of a dog and a human. The forelegs of an insect and the forelimbs of a dog are actually an example of homologous structures, despite initial claims to the contrary. They share a common evolutionary origin and are not analogous structures, despite having similar functions.
