The Krebs cycle, also known as the citric acid cycle, occurs in the inner mitochondrial membrane of human cells. It is a crucial process for cellular respiration, allowing for the breakdown of nutrients and the production of energy-rich molecules, including ATP. The cycle takes place in the mitochondria, specifically inside the inner mitochondrial membrane, and is also known as the tricarboxylic acid cycle.
