A single nucleotide substitution in the first codon position can result in a premature stop codon, leading to a truncated and non-functional protein. This is because the start codon is crucial for initiating translation, and even a small change can disrupt protein synthesis. A substitution in the first position of the start codon would likely have a significant deleterious effect, potentially leading to misreading of the mRNA and a failure to initiate translation.
