A single nucleotide substitution in the first codon position of a gene is likely to result in a premature stop codon, leading to a truncated, non-functional protein. This is because the first position of a codon determines the amino acid identity and a change here can disrupt the entire translation process. The change is a point mutation, which can alter the entire amino acid sequence, potentially leading to a loss of function. A single nucleotide substitution in the first position of the start codon (AUG) would have the greatest deleterious effect, as it would likely result in a premature stop codon and a significant alteration in protein function.
