The Swiss starling population exhibits stabilizing selection, where individuals producing 4-5 eggs per clutch have higher fitness, while those with fewer or more eggs have reduced fitness. The average clutch size of 4-5 eggs is maintained through selective pressures, favoring individuals with this trait and penalizing deviations from it. This results in a stable average value of 4-5 eggs per clutch.
